  "response": "To cap the number of concurrent API calls in a promptfoo evaluation run, you can use the `-j` option to control concurrency. By default, promptfoo evaluates all providers concurrently for each prompt. However, you can limit this concurrency to run evaluations serially or with a specific number of concurrent jobs.\n\nHere’s how you can manage concurrency:\n\n1. **Serial Execution**: To run evaluations serially, using one provider and prompt at a time, you can use the `-j 1` option. This option serializes the target calls, meaning they execute one after another rather than concurrently.\n\n   ```bash\n   promptfoo eval -j 1\n   ```\n\n2. **Limiting Concurrency**: If you want to limit the number of concurrent API calls to a specific number other than one, you can specify that number with the `-j` option. For example, to allow up to 5 concurrent API calls, you would use:\n\n   ```bash\n   promptfoo eval -j 5\n   ```\n\nThese options help you manage the concurrency of API calls during the evaluation process, allowing you to control resource usage and potentially avoid hitting API rate limits.\n\nKeep in mind the ordering caveat mentioned in the context: evaluations marked with `options.runSerially` are executed as a separate partition, which means they do not follow strict test-case order even when running with `-j 1`. Additionally, model-graded assertions may still overlap based on configuration settings like `PROMPTFOO_ASSERTIONS_MAX_CONCURRENCY`.",
